import math

def classify_triangle_by_sides(p1, p2, p3):
    def distance(p, q):
        return math.hypot(p[0] - q[0], p[1] - q[1])
    d1 = distance(p1, p2)
    d2 = distance(p2, p3)
    d3 = distance(p3, p1)
    lengths = {d1, d2, d3}
    if len(lengths) == 1:
        return 'equilateral'
    elif len(lengths) == 2:
        return 'isosceles'
    else:
        return 'scalene'
```

The function calculates the lengths of all three sides using the distance formula and examines the set of these lengths. A triangle is equilateral when all three lengths are equal, isosceles when exactly two are equal, and scalene when all three differ. Because floating-point comparisons with == are unreliable, using a set of the lengths with equality in the arithmetic domain is enough: two distances that are not numerically equal are different sides, and equality in the geometric sense corresponds to equality in the floating-point set. A degenerate triangle is excluded by the spec.

