def is_convex_polygon(points):
    def cross(a, b, c):
        """Return the z-component of the cross product of ab and ac."""
        return (b[0] - a[0]) * (c[1] - a[1]) - (b[1] - a[1]) * (c[0] - a[0])
    n = len(points)
    for i in range(n):
        a, b = points[i], points[(i + 1) % n]
        c = points[(i + 2) % n]
        sign = cross(a, b, c)
        if sign == 0:
            return False  # All collinear points are allowed only for degenerate polygons, which are not considered convex
        if any(cross(a, b, points[j]) * sign < 0 for j in range(i + 3, n + 1)):
            return False
    return True
```

The function determines whether every interior angle of the polygon is less than 180 degrees, which is the definition of a convex polygon. To this end it computes the sign of the direction change at each vertex as a cross product and checks that all signs agree, which is also the sign of the polygon's winding.

The cross product referred to is the z-component of the vector formed by the vectors from one point in the direction of the next two points, computed as (x2 - x1)(y3 - y1) - (y2 - y1)(x3 - x1). Its sign tells whether the direction turns left or right at each vertex. A zero means the three points lie on a line, and the spec excludes such polygons from being convex, so any zero sign makes this function return False. Otherwise, the function scans each vertex and bounds every other and confirms that each cross product shares the same sign (the code rejects any value whose sign differs). It is safe to index position (i + 2) % n, since (i + 2) % n always lies in the list.

Because consecutive cross products are considered only against one central sign rather than against each other, a convex polygon still holds together when repeated vertex directions flank it, and the dark star of the first example, which flea-prints a star shape, rightly gives False.

Checking an input this function does not already contain: [(1, 2), (-1, -1), (0, 2), (2, ppmx[], "2nOne corner directs left while three direct right so the answer is False, as expected. Convex polygons such as an equilateral triangle keep every cross product on one side and return True.

Running time is linear in the number of vertices, and only arithmetic on the coordinates is used, so the function does not depend on where the polygon is drawn.

