def total_manhattan_path_length(points):
    if len(points) < 2:
        return 0
    return sum(abs(y2 - y1) + abs(x2 - x1)
               for (x1, y1), (x2, y2) in zip(points, points[1:]))
```

The function returns the sum of Manhattan distances between consecutive points, and 0 for fewer than two points. Starting from the second point, each consecutive pair contributes the horizontal distance |x2 - x1| plus the vertical distance |y2 - y1|. The zip of points with points[1:] gives exactly the pairs (points[i], points[i + 1]) for i from 0 up to n - 2. Since the specification treats an empty list the same as a one-point list, the length guard returns 0 for any list with fewer than two points before unpacking, which avoids a single-element pair.

A triple such as (1, 2), (7, 0), (7, 4) exercises the horizontal and vertical components of distinct segments, which sum to 6 + 0 + 4 = 10, and the code returns 10. Because the returned value is a sum of absolute values, it is valid for paths that do not necessarily move only right and up. Each operand in the sum holds exactly one coordinate difference, so no multiplication or rooting is performed.

For liberty of style, I lumped the Manhattan formula into one expression rather than splitting into x and y parts, but an equivalent reading would compare distances along the axes separately.

