def nearest_point(target, points):
    def dist2(p):
        x, y = p
        dx = x - target[0]
        dy = y - target[1]
        return dx * dx + dy * dy
    best = points[0]
    for point in points[1:]:
        if dist2(point) < dist2(best):
            best = point
        elif dist2(point) == dist2(best):
            best = point
    return best
```

The function uses squared distance instead of Euclidean distance, which is acceptable because squaring preserves the order of magnitudes. It keeps the closest point seen so far, moving to a new one whenever the squared distance is smaller, and replacing it on a tie, so the first closest point in the input list is kept. The last seen value is returned at the end.

