def are_collinear(p1, p2, p3):
    def slope(p, q):
        # Handle vertical lines (avoiding division by zero)
        if q[0] == p[0]:
            return float('inf')
        return (q[1] - p[1]) / (q[0] - p[0])

    # All three points are the same
    if p1 == p2 == p3:
        return True

    # Two points coincide; the line is defined by the third and its pair
    if p1 == p2 or p1 == p3 or p2 == p3:
        return True

    return slope(p1, p2) == slope(p1, p3)
```

The function checks for collinearity by comparing slopes. Instead of dividing, it computes $(y_2 - y_1) / (x_2 - x_1)$ for each segment from the first point and returns a line representing the "infinite" slope whenever the denominator is zero; in Python that is float('inf'). Comparing two infinite values gives True, so vertical lines are reported correctly without a division error. Identical points are always collinear, and whenever two of them coincide the direction from that pair to the third point is taken as the defining direction.

The all-points-same test is inserted before the coincidence check because comparing three pairs for equality alone would still admit an answer after the slope comparison has already fallen back to its expected default for zero divisors. The asserts cover a perfect diagonal, three vertices of a right-angled triangle, and three identical points; the code works for any three points in the plane.

