The document and a redo pointer, which keeps the top of a one-item stack, are maintained as the commands arrive. A do appends and resets the pointer, undo removes the last item and records it under the pointer, and redo appends that recorded item and clears the pointer. Since redo only moves once recorded value back and undo only ever records one value, the pair of operations is never malformed, and an empty redo pointer has nothing to append. The empty stack then has no effect, which is the result the specification describes for every 'do', 'undo' and 'redo'.

